Z||000000||buds||07-13-2000||09:16 AM||||Im almost finished my v8 install.my question is has any tried using the stock rad or do i have to go to the 4 core.my engine is a completely rebuilt 1990 305 tpi.should i try it with my rad or not even bother?? thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||lennyb4||07-13-2000||09:50 AM||||The standard two core rad will not work (you'll run wayyyy to hot). Batboy has a V-8 in his fiero, he said using the 4 core rad it runs just a little higher then stock. I would not even try it with your factory rad. Check around for a four core, i know V-8 Archie sells them (kinda expensive though) and im sure you can find the right one other places to or have one made at a good rad shop.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smallPegasus.gif">   <BR>87 Coupe... Got rice???<p>[This message has been edited by lennyb4 (edited 07-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||07-13-2000||09:53 AM||||It's Batboy with the Batmobile that has the V-8 not Monkeyman. The Monkeymobile has went to Fiero Heaven and out of the ashes came  The Grape Ape.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 07-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||HarryG||07-13-2000||12:25 PM||||If I were you, and I had a stock V6 radiator in good condition, I'd try that first. Archie talks about radiators in his V8 Install FAQ section:  some of his customers have tried to use stock radiators "...with mixed results, depending on where they live".<BR>If you lived in Texas and used the AC all the time, I'd say don't even try it, but since you don't, what have you got to lose?  I'm assuming you're not going to jump in and try to go cross-county without sorting it out first!  Also heard of someone using an S-10 narrowed core with Fiero ends to give a 3-core version.  Cost was lower than the 4-core.<P>BTW, are you using a kit or is this a DIY swap?  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||buds||07-13-2000||04:30 PM||||Does any one know if there is direct bolt in 4 core rad from another car,truck,suv etc that would help me with the cooling problems I might encounter??<BR>Thank for the replys so far eh.<BR>                                 ||||reg||5
Z||000005||mrfixit58||07-13-2000||05:01 PM||||Hey buds,<P>Welcom to the forum. Regurding radiators... My opinion is your radiator is one place that is best NOT to cut corners. Years ago I installed a SBC into a Chevy LUV truck. I spent hours and hours searching junk yards to find a close bolt-in replacement. <P>I did finally come close but it was a constant maintenance problem. Every time I had it repaired, it would pop another place. And, on top of it all, it never really cooled very well. <P>After a couple of years and MANY overheated days later, I finally had a 4-core built to fit. The repair man used my tanks and installed the core. It cost me $125. Best money I spent. <P>Unfortunatly, because I ran the motor too hot too many times and it went south before it's time (cooked the cam bearings). My advice, take your radiator down to your favorite radiator man and ask if he can build you one that will fit into the same size hole. Or, look at the generic aluminum radiators and see if you can make someting to hold it in place. <P>I would hate for you to go to all the problem of installing the V-8, only to have to keep it parked for "cool days only".<P>For what it's worth,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||07-13-2000||07:17 PM||||Actually, with my big radiator, the Batmobile never runs hot. Steady 180 degrees all the time. My cooling fan kicks on at 160 degrees, so it's on most of the time though.||||reg||1
